## Summary

Security audit of main at 64d444ff (workflow Audit, stage Security Audit). Read the trust model (`docs/audits/security.md`, `docs/dev.md`), the two prior security-fix commits and the earlier audit tasks so nothing was refiled, then the code at every boundary: sign-in, sessions and bearer tokens, Spoof Mode, every `auth.Public` path (mail and delivery hooks, calendar watch, GitHub and build hooks, feeds, unsubscribe, outside invitations, MCP OAuth), the write authorizer and policies, each `/api/do/` route, blob serving, share cards, mail in and out, server-side fetches, Claude's inputs, the browser's HTML and navigation sinks and local storage, and the deploy and logging paths.

One real finding, fixed:
- Before: Helios When's saved-calendar page was addressed by the saved search's feed token (`/c/<token>`), the same secret that opens the public feed `/open/feed/<token>.ics`, which runs the search as its owner; a copied, bookmarked or link-previewed page address handed out the owner's events, invite-only ones included, and the household's going/waitlisted standing.
- Now: the page is at `/c/<saved search ID>` (`my-heliosian` for the default), as every other row's page uses its ID; the client identifies a calendar by `key` and the token appears only inside the feed address under Subscribe. Old `/c/<token>` links show Not here (deliberately not kept as a second path); a browser's remembered active calendar resets once.

Verified with `go vet`, the app package tests, and sample-server captures of `/c/sav00000000001` and Heliosian's home. Review: one round, no findings. Main merged cleanly afterwards (one unrelated change to `internal/db/query.go`), vet and tests rerun.

Noticed and left: `/api/do/thumbnail` writes an unreferenced JPEG to the bucket before the policy refuses a member (swept later; a fix would be one call-site check); list managers can claim any unclaimed `ALIAS` name for their own list; team admins' `REDIRECT` rows can send unclaimed portal paths off-site before sign-in (the documented feature); and everything the trust model allows on purpose (public share cards by ID, `person-at`, super admins and Spoof Mode, OAuth redirects to any https host, tokens in the request log).

